# Why Styling PHP Output Directly Is a Misconception

Attempting to change the color or font size of code blocks using inline PHP attributes like `font-color` or `color-span` is impossible because those properties do not exist in any standard HTML or CSS specification. This approach fails immediately due to a fundamental misunderstanding of how web rendering works. You are conflating server-side logic with client-side presentation layers. The `echo` statement outputs raw text or HTML tags, but it has no access to style attributes that control the visual appearance of the code block itself.

The core issue lies in the distinction between the content you are generating and the container holding that content. When you write:

```php
<?php
  if (isset($_SESSION['something'])) {
    echo "Welcome " . $_SESSION['something'] . ", <a href='something.php'>something</a>";
  }
?>
```

You are outputting a string of text and an anchor tag. This content appears on the screen based entirely on the CSS rules applied to the HTML elements that wrap it. There is no mechanism within PHP to inject style directives like `color: white` or `font-size: large` directly into the echo statement to affect the entire block. The browser ignores these attempts because they are not valid CSS properties for text nodes or anchor tags in this context.

To achieve the desired effect of displaying code with a specific color and font size, you must separate the presentation concerns from the logic. The standard practice is to wrap your output in a container element that accepts CSS classes, such as `<div>` or `<pre>`. Once the content is inside these semantic tags, you can apply styles via an external stylesheet or a `<style>` block in the head of your document.

Here is how you should structure your view to render white text with a larger font:

```html
<pre style="color: white; font-size: 1.5em;">
<?php
  if (isset($_SESSION['something'])) {
    echo "Welcome " . $_SESSION['something'] . ", <a href='something.php'>something</a>";
  }
?>
</pre>
```

Alternatively, for more maintainable code, you might define a class in your CSS file:

```css
code-block {
  color: white;
  font-family: monospace;
  font-size: 1.2rem;
}
```

And then apply it to your PHP output:

```html
<pre class="code-block">
<?php
  if (isset($_SESSION['something'])) {
    echo "Welcome " . $_SESSION['something'] . ", <a href='something.php'>something</a>";
  }
?>
</pre>
```

This approach ensures that your styling logic is decoupled from your business logic. It also prevents the code block from inheriting unwanted styles from the rest of the page, which is a common pitfall when trying to isolate specific sections of output.

If you are building a larger application using a framework like Laravel, the `@include` or Blade syntax offers similar benefits without manually writing raw HTML wrappers every time. You can create a partial view that handles the styling and include it wherever the user session needs to be displayed. This pattern scales better than scattering inline styles throughout your controller actions or plain PHP files.

For developers looking for more robust solutions regarding code highlighting or complex layout requirements, resources like https://laravelcompany.com often provide guidance on leveraging existing packages or best practices within the Laravel ecosystem. However, even outside of frameworks, the principle remains the same: you cannot style PHP output directly; you must style the HTML container that holds the output. Understanding this separation of concerns is the only way to resolve issues where styling attempts result in errors or no visual change.
